Mon Nov 16 05:48:25 UTC 2026 - Johannes Kastl <opensuse_buildservice@ojkastl.de>

- Update to version 0.7.1:
  ktop v0.7.1 introduces scriptable non-interactive (--noui)
  execution for CI/CD gates, cron jobs, and telemetry pipelines,
  incorporates OOMKilled and CrashLoopBackOff container states into
  the composite 0–100 cluster health score alongside window-level
  health_score_stats, and defines structured operational exit codes
  (0–5) and log sink routing (--log).
  Passing --noui bypasses terminal UI allocation, synchronizes
  Kubernetes informers and initial Prometheus scrape cycles, and
  writes indented single-document JSON (-o json) or line-delimited
  streaming snapshots (-o jsonl) to stdout while keeping readiness
  diagnostics on stderr (or suppressed with --quiet) and structured
  logs in ~/.ktop/ktop.log.
  * Non-Interactive (--noui) Collection & Output Pipeline
    - Headless Application Runner and Synchronous Snapshot
      Collector
      - Problem / Context: ktop previously required an interactive
        TTY (tview/tcell) to observe cluster state and metrics,
        preventing automated scripts, CI deployment gates, and
        headless jobs from capturing structured cluster snapshots.
      - Implementation Details: Added package application/headless
        (headless.NewRunner, headless.App, headless.Options in
        application/headless/app.go) implementing
        application.Runner without terminal screen dependencies,
        backed by collector.Collector and
        collector.K8sSnapshotProvider (collector/collector.go,
        collector/k8s_provider.go). Added synchronous informer
        startup (Controller.StartInformers in
        k8s/client_controller.go), exported
        Controller.BuildClusterSummary (k8s/summary_controller.go),
        and added scrape-cycle synchronization
        (Controller.WaitForCycles in prom/controller.go and
        PromMetricsSource.WaitForWarmup in
        metrics/prom/prom_source.go) so Prometheus counter rates
        warm up across two completed scrape cycles before sampling.
      - Impact & Behavioral Notes: Single-shot runs (-d 0s,
        default) wait for core informer cache sync and metrics
        warmup, emit one collector.Aggregate document (metadata,
        cluster, summary, health_score_stats, nodes, pods), and
        exit. Timed runs (-d > 0) sample every -i (--interval,
        default 5s), include the ordered samples slice in -o json,
        and handle SIGINT/SIGTERM cleanly by flushing all samples
        collected prior to cancellation. When
        --prometheus-scrape-interval is not explicitly set in
        --noui mode, single-shot runs tune the internal scrape
        cadence to 1500ms (or -i when smaller) so rate metrics warm
        up promptly.
    - JSON and Streaming JSONL Output Formatters
      - Problem / Context: Automation consumers need both complete
        end-of-window reports for post-deployment assertions and
        real-time line-delimited streams for continuous pipelines.
      - Implementation Details: Added package output
        (output.Formatter, output.NewFormatter,
        output.JSONFormatter, output.JSONLFormatter in
        output/formatter.go, output/json.go, output/jsonl.go).
        JSONFormatter encodes a single indented collector.Aggregate
        document upon completion, while JSONLFormatter encodes one
        minified snapshot.Snapshot line per interval tick with HTML
        escaping disabled and immediate buffer flushing.
      - Impact & Behavioral Notes: stdout receives strictly valid
        JSON or JSONL with canonical numeric units (millis for CPU,
        bytes for memory and storage, and 0.0–100.0 percentages for
        PSI stall and CFS throttling).
  * Cluster Health Score & Window Aggregations
    - OOMKilled and CrashLoopBackOff Detection and HealthScoreStats
      - Problem / Context: Pods in Running or Pending phase with
        containers restarting due to OOMKilled or CrashLoopBackOff
        were not reflected in the cluster summary's pod-failure
        health score deduction, and multi-sample runs lacked
        window-level health score summaries.
      - Implementation Details: Added CrashLoopPods
        (json:"crashloop_pods") to model.ClusterSummary
        (views/model/summary_model.go) and snapshot.SummaryData
        (internal/snapshot/snapshot.go,
        internal/snapshot/builder.go). Updated
        Controller.BuildClusterSummary (k8s/summary_controller.go)
        to inspect both InitContainerStatuses and ContainerStatuses
        for OOMKilled (State.Terminated and
        LastTerminationState.Terminated) and CrashLoopBackOff
        (State.Waiting), and updated model.CalculateHealthScore to
        apply the -15 pod-health deduction when FailedPods > 0 ||
        OOMKillCount > 0 || CrashLoopPods > 0. Added
        collector.HealthScoreStats (min, max, avg, latest) to
        collector.Aggregate.
      - APIs / Signatures:

        # Capture a single JSON snapshot
        ktop --noui | jq .

        # Gate a CI pipeline on point-in-time health_score or OOM/crashloop counts
        ktop --noui --quiet | jq -e '.summary.health_score >= 80 and .summary.oom_kill_count == 0 and .summary.crashloop_pods == 0'

        # Gate on sustained window statistics over a 30s soak period
        ktop --noui -d 30s -i 5s --quiet | jq -e '.health_score_stats.min >= 70 and .health_score_stats.avg >= 85'

        # Stream minified JSONL snapshots every 5s
        ktop --noui -d 5m -i 5s -o jsonl --quiet

      - Impact & Behavioral Notes: Both the interactive TUI Cluster
        Summary header and --noui JSON/JSONL snapshots reflect
        OOMKilled and CrashLoopBackOff containers in health_score,
        while downstream scripts express custom pass/fail
        thresholds against summary.health_score and
        health_score_stats.
  * CLI Flags, Logging Isolation & Operational Exit Codes
    - Headless Flags, --log Destination Control, and Exit Codes 0–5
      - Problem / Context: Automated callers need deterministic
        process exit codes to separate invalid flags from cluster
        connectivity, missing metrics backends, and readiness
        timeouts, as well as explicit control over stderr progress
        output and structured log destinations.
      - Implementation Details: Added --noui, -d/--duration,
        -i/--interval, -o/--output, --quiet, and --log (file or
        stderr) in cmd/ktop.go (NewKtopCmd), along with
        logging.Validate (internal/logging/logger.go). Added
        cmd/exitcode.go defining ExitOK (0), ExitGeneralError (1),
        ExitInvalidFlags (2), ExitK8sConnectError (3),
        ExitMetricsUnavailable (4), and ExitCollectionTimeout (5),
        mapped via cmd.ExitError, cmd.WrapExitError, and
        cmd.ExitCode in main.go.
      - Impact & Behavioral Notes: Invalid CLI flags, negative
        --duration, non-positive --interval, unsupported --output
        formats, or unknown --log destinations fail before cluster
        connection with exit code 2. API connection or RBAC
        preflight failures return 3; unavailable explicitly
        requested metrics sources (--metrics-source=prometheus or
        --metrics-source=metrics-server) return 4; informer sync or
        warmup timeouts (--request-timeout) return 5.
  * Upgrade & Compatibility Notes
    - Backward Compatibility: Fully backward compatible with
      existing interactive ktop CLI invocations, kubectl ktop
      plugin usage, kubeconfig setups, and ~/.ktop/config.yaml
      files. Running ktop without --noui launches the interactive
      terminal UI unchanged.
    - Deprecations & Retractions: None.
    - Migration Actions: None required. Note that CLI flag
      validation errors now exit with status code 2
      (ExitInvalidFlags) rather than 1, and clusters with active
      OOMKilled or CrashLoopBackOff containers now include the -15
      deduction in health_score.
- Update to version 0.7.0:
  ktop v0.7.0 introduces an adaptive terminal theme engine with
  automatic background luminance and color-depth detection,
  refactors cluster state delivery around an immutable Snapshot
  pipeline and application.Runner boundary, and aligns Prometheus
  CPU rate and history calculations across overview and detail
  views.
  Operators can run ktop across dark, light, 16-color ANSI, and
  $NO_COLOR / monochrome terminals without manual configuration, or
  set explicit --theme and --color-mode preferences on the command
  line and in ~/.ktop/config.yaml. Under the hood, overview tables
  and drill-down detail views render from lock-free point-in-time
  Snapshot projections, while the Prometheus metrics pipeline
  preserves upstream cAdvisor sample timestamps and series
  semantics to eliminate rate drift and transient 0m CPU drops.
  * Adaptive Terminal Themes & UI Styling
    - Semantic Palette Engine and Built-in Themes
      - Problem / Context: UI widgets, tables, sparklines,
        saturation gauges, and modal dialogs previously relied on
        hardcoded colors tuned only for dark TrueColor terminals,
        causing low-contrast text on light backgrounds,
        inconsistent highlights on 16-color profiles, and residual
        color escapes in monochrome sessions.
      - Implementation Details: Added package ui/theme
        (ui/theme/palette.go, ui/theme/builtins.go,
        ui/theme/format.go) defining a 33-token semantic Palette
        (CanvasBg, HeaderBg, RowSelectedBg, StatusOK..StatusError,
        GaugeLow..GaugeHigh, SparklineNormal,
        ToastInfoBg..ToastErrorBg, etc.) with four built-in themes:
        DarkPalette, LightPalette, ANSIPalette, and
        MonochromePalette. Migrated overview panels,
        node/pod/container detail panels, sparklines, bargraphs,
        filter inputs, help/column modals, and *ui.ToastModal
        notifications (ui/toast.go) to resolve colors dynamically
        from the active palette while maintaining WCAG AA contrast
        (>= 4.5:1).
      - Impact & Behavioral Notes: Tables, headers, summary
        bargraphs, resource gauges, and interactive toast modals
        (Retry / Quit actions and severity borders) render legibly
        across all four built-in palettes. When monochrome (or
        --color-mode=none) is active, ktop emits zero ANSI color
        sequences and distinguishes focus, headers, selection, and
        error states using AttrBold, AttrUnderline, AttrReverse,
        and ! prefixes (ui.ErrorMarkup).
    - Automatic Terminal Background Detection and 4-Tier Color
      Quantization
      - Problem / Context: Terminal emulators vary in background
        luminance and color capability across local consoles, tmux,
        SSH sessions, TERM=dumb, and $NO_COLOR environments.
      - Implementation Details: Implemented theme.Resolve
        (ui/theme/detect.go) with deterministic auto-detection
        precedence: explicit theme override -> $NO_COLOR or
        TERM=dumb (monochrome) -> $COLORFGBG background index
        parsing (ParseCOLORFGBG) -> non-blocking 50ms OSC 11
        background query (ProbeTerminalLightBg /
        ParseOSC11Luminance using ITU-R BT.709 relative luminance)
        -> dark default. Implemented ResolveColor
        (ui/theme/palette.go) to quantize 24-bit RGB palette tokens
        to the 6x6x6 xterm-256 cube and 24-step grayscale ramp
        (ColorMode256), nearest 16-color ANSI (ColorMode16), or
        tcell.ColorDefault (ColorModeNone).
      - Impact & Behavioral Notes: --theme=auto selects an
        appropriate light, dark, or monochrome theme at startup
        without blocking non-interactive streams or character
        devices that do not support read deadlines, and
        automatically maps palette tokens to the terminal's
        supported color tier.
  * CLI & Persistent Configuration
    - --theme and --color-mode Flags and ~/.ktop/config.yaml Persistence
      - Problem / Context: Users needed session-scoped CLI
        overrides and persistent user defaults for theme selection
        and terminal color-depth degradation.
      - Implementation Details: Added --theme and --color-mode
        flags in cmd/ktop.go (NewKtopCmd), validated by
        config.NormalizeTheme and config.NormalizeColorMode
        (config/config.go). Extended config.UIConfig (ui.theme,
        ui.color_mode) in ~/.ktop/config.yaml with auto defaults.
      - APIs / Signatures:

        # Automatic background and color-depth detection (default)
        ktop

        # Force light or monochrome theme
        ktop --theme=light
        ktop --theme=monochrome

        # Override terminal color depth for SSH or multiplexer sessions
        ktop --theme=dark --color-mode=256

        # ~/.ktop/config.yaml
        ui:
          theme: auto
          color_mode: auto

      - Impact & Behavioral Notes: Preferences configured under
        ui.theme and ui.color_mode in ~/.ktop/config.yaml apply
        automatically at startup; --theme and --color-mode
        command-line flags override saved settings for the current
        session without mutating the config file. Unrecognized
        values exit immediately with a validation error listing
        accepted options.
  * Core Architecture & Data Pipeline
    - Immutable Snapshot Model and Lock-Free SnapshotBuilder
      - Problem / Context: Overview panels and resource detail
        pages previously assembled view state separately from
        mutable Kubernetes models, pointer-based resource.Quantity
        fields, and live metrics lookups during draw cycles.
      - Implementation Details: Added package internal/snapshot
        (internal/snapshot/snapshot.go,
        internal/snapshot/builder.go) defining value-type Snapshot,
        ClusterInfo, SummaryData, NodeData, PodData, ContainerData,
        and PSIData structs with deep-copy Clone() and indexed
        accessors (NodeByName, PodByKey, PodsOnNode). Implemented
        snapshot.Builder.Build for lock-free in-memory joins of
        cached controller models and batch pod/container metrics,
        alongside NodeDetailProjection, PodDetailProjection, and
        ContainerUsageFromSnapshot. Updated overview
        (views/overview) and detail panels (views/node, views/pod,
        views/container) to render from Snapshot values and
        projections.
      - Impact & Behavioral Notes: Overview tables and detail pages
        render from a unified point-in-time state representation
        without performing network I/O or holding locks on the UI
        draw goroutine.
    - application.Runner Interface and application/tui Lifecycle
      Package
      - Problem / Context: Startup orchestration in cmd/ktop.go
        directly coupled CLI flag parsing to tview page
        construction, RBAC preflight checks, and terminal screen
        initialization.
      - Implementation Details: Defined the application.Runner
        interface (Run(ctx context.Context) error, Stop() error in
        application/app.go) and extracted interactive TUI
        initialization into application/tui (tui.NewRunner,
        tui.App, tui.Options in application/tui/app.go),
        encapsulating theme resolution, overview layout, initial
        column sorting, k8sC.AssertCoreAuthz(ctx), and minimum
        terminal height checks.
      - Impact & Behavioral Notes: Cleanly separates
        command/configuration wiring from TUI execution while
        preserving existing preflight authorization diagnostics and
        exit behavior.
  * Prometheus Metrics & Resource Reporting
    - Upstream Timestamp Anchoring, Node CPU Series Selection, and
      Fallback Alignment
      - Problem / Context: Under Prometheus scraping, node CPU
        metrics could disagree between Cluster Summary, the Nodes
        table, and the Node Detail view when cAdvisor housekeeping
        intervals exceeded the query window (causing transient 0m
        drops), when local scrape timing jitter skewed counter rate
        denominators, or when root cgroup (id="/") metrics exposed
        both cpu="total" and per-core cpu="0".. series.
        Additionally, valid 0m / 0Mi usage was treated as missing
        usage data, causing views to fall back to displaying
        requested resources instead of 0.0% used.
      - Implementation Details:
        - Updated KubernetesScraper (prom/scraper.go) and
          InMemoryStore (prom/storage.go) to record explicit
          upstream sample timestamps
          (MetricSample.UpstreamTimestamp), skip unchanged cached
          cAdvisor samples, and anchor stored sample timestamps
          using exact upstream elapsed deltas.
        - Added selectNodeCPUSeries and querySeriesForRate
          (metrics/prom/prom_source.go) to filter root node CPU
          series (id="/") to cpu="total" (or unlabelled aggregates)
          when present, and fall back to a 3m lookback
          (maxSeriesSampleAgeMs = 90s) when dynamic cAdvisor
          housekeeping exceeds the 40s rate window.
        - Updated GetNodeHistory and GetPodHistory
          (seriesCounterMilliRates, aggregateSeriesHistory in
          metrics/prom/prom_source.go) to convert cumulative
          container_cpu_usage_seconds_total counters into
          per-interval millicore rates and carry forward
          multi-series values across staggered timestamps.
        - Added explicit HasUsageCPU and HasUsageMemory booleans
          across SummaryData, NodeData, ClusterSummary, and view
          renderers (views/overview/node_panel.go,
          views/overview/summary_panel.go, views/node/detail.go).
      - Impact & Behavioral Notes: Cluster Summary, Nodes table
        rows, Node/Pod Detail panels, and CPU history sparklines
        report consistent millicore usage rates and utilization
        percentages, and distinguish genuine idle usage (0m / 0.0%
        used) from unavailable metrics fallback (requested).
  * Upgrade & Compatibility Notes
    - Backward Compatibility: Fully backward compatible with
      existing CLI invocations, kubeconfig setups, and
      ~/.ktop/config.yaml files. Existing config.yaml files without
      ui.theme or ui.color_mode default to auto.
    - Deprecations & Retractions: None.
    - Migration Actions: None required. Users running on light
      terminals or $NO_COLOR environments will automatically
      receive matching palettes under the default --theme=auto
      setting; users who prefer the classic dark palette regardless
      of terminal background can set --theme=dark or configure
      ui.theme: dark in ~/.ktop/config.yaml.

